No, you have not!
Your description of what to expect from na.omit is correct.

What you have misunderstood is how model.frame is intended to be used. 
model.frame is meant to process formulas or fits, as is said in the 
title of its help page, ?model.frame.

model.frame does remove NA's, if any. And when it does so it returns an 
object with attribute "na.action" set.

But if there are no NA's then model.frame does not keep an eventual 
"na.action" attribute because formulas and fits can be anything and the 
data set it processes and passes on to na.omit is not the data set in 
its argument `data` like I explained earlier.

Hope this helps,

Rui Barradas

